WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.469
-Our first guest tonight
is the award-winning host

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:03.703
of "The Graham Norton Show."

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.872
His new novel, "Home Stretch,"
is available now.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.841
Please welcome back to the show
the legendary Graham Norton.

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:10.510
How you are, Graham?

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.279
-Oh, I'm so well.
Lovely to see you again, Seth.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.581
Hi.
-It's lovely to see you, too.

00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:18.451
It has also been lovely
to have you back in your studio.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.853
I think we both returned around
the same time in the fall.

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:24.023
How has life been for you guys?
Because you've kind of gone

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.926
back and forth, as far as what
you've had for an audience.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.427
-Yeah.
When we came back,

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:31.097
we had a sort of
socially distanced audience.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.833
Which is kind of worse
than no audience.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:37.871
Because we went from 600 to 60,
so the applause, it sounded like

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:39.638
someone emptying
a bucket of fish.

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:42.341
It was just like nothing.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:46.446
And as a performer, your
relationship with the audience,

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:49.515
I don't think you should ever
feel sorry for the audience.

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:51.918
And that's how you felt.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:54.320
Their masks
just dotted across the thing.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.656
So they're gone.
They -- They got banned.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.025
And then it was just us
and the crew.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.860
Which I thought
would be terrible.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.562
But, actually,
you feel a bit giddy

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:06.733
when it's just you
and the crew in the room.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.402
And I don't know if you agree,
but there's something about

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.038
if you get a laugh
out of a cameraman,

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.708
it equals about getting
450 people laughing.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.243
Regular people.
-I agree with you 100%,

00:01:18.243 --> 00:01:20.813
except for our cameraman Buck,
who laughs at everything.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:22.882
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, okay.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:24.884
Good hire.
-So we have, like, one guy

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.953
where it's like --
it's meaningless.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.587
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:30.656
But it is -- I will agree
with you on the giddiness level.

00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:33.126
-Crew humor.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:34.660
-Oh, my God,
the amount of pandering

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.697
I've been doing to the crew.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.965
I mean,
when I have an audience back,

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:41.801
the crew is gonna turn on them
the way they do

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.803
when, like,
a new boyfriend moves in.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.039
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.941
-"Do jokes
about my hometown again!"

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.776
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.312
-You know, one of the things
I want to ask about, you know,

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:55.781
because you have -- you have
developed, you have perfected

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:59.819
this sort of party atmosphere
with your guests.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.454
You know, this last year, and
certainly without an audience,

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:04.057
it maybe feels
a little bit less like a party.

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:06.559
Have you managed to maintain it?

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.194
-Well, I was worried
'cause I thought,

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.663
"Who would want to come
to the studio anymore?"

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.965
'Cause we can have
some guests in the studio.

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:15.068
Turns out everybody.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:16.969
People were showing up, like,
in ridiculous --

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:20.172
like in ball gowns
because they hadn't got to wear

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:22.341
their fancy dressing-up clothes

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.043
for months and months
and months.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.912
And, you know,
at the end when I'm saying,

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.146
"And thank you to my guests,"

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:30.016
you can see them going,
"Do we have to leave now?

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.319
I have to go back to the house,
to those people I've been

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:36.255
looking at every day for the
last, you know, 12 months?"

00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:39.292
So that was kind of an upside.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.994
The Zoom thing --
this thing, you know --

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:44.030
I think we've all
got better at it.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.031
But it's still not ideal.
You know?

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:47.300
I wish I was there.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.068
And, you know,
you're talking to a screen.

00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:51.537
And it's all a bit weird.

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:54.573
But it's better than nothing,
I guess.

00:02:54.573 --> 00:02:55.741
-I do think
it's better than nothing.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:57.910
I do think
it's not maybe terrible.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.912
-The reviews are in --
"Better than nothing."

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:01.380
[ Laughter ]

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.417
-I do -- I don't think it's
terrible for people at home.

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.519
And maybe I should let them
speak to that,

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.554
because I don't watch shows
like this.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.724
But I feel like -- at least
if you're home right now, like,

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.692
Graham Norton
is looking right at you.

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:15.828
And I think that there's
some value to that.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.730
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.701
I think if you're watching at
home, it has got much better.

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.503
I think in the beginning,
when none of us

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:28.675
knew what we were doing,
it was quite a difficult watch.

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:30.910
-Yes.
-But it has --

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.779
It has got much more fluid.
And, actually, we got to a point

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:36.548
where, you know,
the people in the studio

00:03:36.548 --> 00:03:38.351
would talk to the person
on the Zoom screen.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:41.086
The other person on the Zoom
screen would know

00:03:41.086 --> 00:03:42.889
that their friend
was sitting in the studio,

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:44.590
so they'd talk to them.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.160
And again, you know,
a bit odd in the room,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.661
but on TV,
that probably worked fine.

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:52.097
It didn't bother anybody.
-Right.

00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:54.300
I also think it was helpful
that all audiences

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.801
understood why we were doing it.

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:58.871
It's one of the few upsides to
it being a worldwide pandemic.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:01.707
I think that that helped
that everybody knew the details.

00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:03.609
Hey, this is your --
I'm very impressed by this,

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:04.911
and a little bit jealous,

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:07.880
that you've managed to write
three novels.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.716
And this one
is about a car crash

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:13.186
and then the secrets
that the people involved in it

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:14.587
carry with them.

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:17.223
It's maybe a little bit more
serious, the subject matter,

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.225
than people
would expect from you.

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:20.559
Was that one of
the exciting things

00:04:20.559 --> 00:04:23.129
about taking it on
as a subject matter?

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.930
-It was, really.
I think with the novels,

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.833
what I wanted to do was kind of
take myself out of them.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:29.435
Because you know, as a reader,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.470
I think it's
really intimate entertainment.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.505
It's just you and that page
with the marks.

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:35.108
You get lost in the story.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.209
Whereas if Graham Norton
off the TV is kind of

00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:40.646
reading over your shoulder,
that's a bit distracting.

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:45.918
So, I wanted the books to be
as far removed from the TV show,

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.487
from my TV persona, as possible.

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:52.624
And I've ended up writing
really serious books.

00:04:52.624 --> 00:04:55.094
This one, it starts quite dark.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:57.897
I mean, you know,
it's clearly a car crash.

00:04:57.897 --> 00:05:01.667
Three people die.
It's not a laugh riot.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:04.003
Quite funeral-heavy
for the first few chapters.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:07.273
But then it does open up.
And Connor,

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:09.108
the central character,
who's driving the car,

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:11.877
he ends up, you know,
living in Liverpool, in London,

00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:13.646
and finally
makes his home in New York.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:16.315
So there's some American
in this book, as well.

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:18.051
-And this was true of you.

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:19.952
This is obviously not
autobiographical,

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:23.055
but you went from Ireland
to New York for a bit.

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.390
How was your time in New York?

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.727
-[ Laughs ]
I was 20.

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:30.329
This was --
I was an Irish 20

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:34.934
in the early '80s, which makes
me kind of an international 14.

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:37.670
And we showed up --
we showed up in New York,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.940
this little gaggle,
I mean green in every sense

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.342
we were, and I think --

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:45.077
Do you remember the TV show
"The Equalizer"...

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:46.211
-Yes, of course.
-...with Edward Woodward?

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:48.647
-With Edward --
-Edward Woodward.

00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:50.549
-Yes.
-Eewa Woowa. Yeah.

00:05:50.549 --> 00:05:53.119
And, uh --
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:54.686
And I think I watched
too much of that,

00:05:54.686 --> 00:05:58.790
because I got to New York,
and I was just terrified

00:05:58.790 --> 00:05:59.892
the whole time.

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:01.093
You know,
if two people were talking,

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:03.362
that was a drug deal going bad.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.931
You know?
So it was all terrible.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:09.701
And then, to make matters worse,
the morning after we got there,

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:12.872
we had, I think it was called
orientation or something.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.507
I don't think it was a cop
gave this talk,

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.008
but somebody gave this talk.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:20.279
And it was essentially how not
to get killed in New York City.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.448
[ Laughter ]
And we were all like...

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.483
And I still --
It's so engrained in me

00:06:24.483 --> 00:06:25.885
I still obey the rules.

00:06:25.885 --> 00:06:28.755
Like, in New York City,
never look up.

00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:32.258
I mean, if you look up, that's
tantamount to a death wish.

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:34.160
-Right?
-It's one away from suicide

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.429
if you look up in New York City.

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:39.064
Never walk next to the wall,
always walk on the curb --

00:06:39.064 --> 00:06:40.333
on the curb side.

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:43.836
You know, all these things,
and I still do them.

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:45.404
-Well, I mean, and here you are.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.039
You're obviously
living a very vibrant life.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:48.674
-I survived.
-So it's paid off.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.109
-Yeah, yeah.
-We talked about your mom

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:52.311
last time she was here --
last time you were here.

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:54.847
She's not been here yet.
Impossible to book your mom.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.081
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:58.951
But you -- how does she feel
about your novels?

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:00.953
Do you share them with her
through the process,

00:07:00.953 --> 00:07:02.588
or do you wait till it's done?

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:05.257
-I wait till it's done.
And my mother's a big reader,

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:07.927
so I always feel like
if she doesn't like them,

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.162
then I failed.

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:13.232
This book had --
you know, it had some sex in it.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:15.868
So, I go, "Oh, Mom, I'm not sure
you're gonna like this one."

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.369
And so she didn't read it
for a long time

00:07:17.369 --> 00:07:20.673
because she was scared
she wouldn't like it.

00:07:20.673 --> 00:07:23.943
Because my mother,
she, you know -- she's quite --

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:26.045
she tells you in her own way.

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:29.882
Like if she watches the show,
my TV show, and thinks it's bad,

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:32.751
next time we talk on the phone,
she'll go, "Hmm.

00:07:32.751 --> 00:07:37.389
How do you think it went?"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:39.792
I think we know
how you think it went.

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.928
So, in the end, she read it,
and she did like it.

00:07:42.928 --> 00:07:44.563
She went, "Why didn't you think
I'd like it?"

00:07:44.563 --> 00:07:47.266
And I went,
"Well, there's sex in it."

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:49.268
And she went,
"I know about sex."

00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:51.671
And I was like, "Ew!
Please stop talking."

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.239
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.239 --> 00:07:55.340
"I liked it before --
I liked it before

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.576
when I thought you didn't.
-You --

00:07:57.576 --> 00:07:59.712
You've always had
a very high level --

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:02.014
we're used to a very high level
of celebrity on your show.

00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:06.585
But you almost got what would be
considered maybe the peak.

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:09.388
You almost got some royals
on your show,

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:11.190
or at least were led to believe
you were close.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.992
-Yeah.
There was a rumor went out that

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:15.494
we were gonna get --
I think it was Kate and William.

00:08:15.494 --> 00:08:17.529
And we were so excited.

00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:19.664
Because, you know,
they're young people.

00:08:19.664 --> 00:08:20.866
They're in the world.

00:08:20.866 --> 00:08:23.168
It's possible they would
come on a talk show.

00:08:23.168 --> 00:08:26.171
So, we were very excited.
Where this rumor came from,

00:08:26.171 --> 00:08:29.041
we were asking our booker, he
didn't know anything about it.

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:32.144
And we were asking around.
I think he contacted

00:08:32.144 --> 00:08:34.747
their press office to say,
"Where did this come from?"

00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:36.715
Yeah, the rumor came from me.

00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:41.253
I had started the rumor
in an interview where I said,

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:42.822
"Quite nice if they came on."

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:45.024
And then somebody else
reported that,

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:48.027
and, so, yeah, to much
disappointment all around.

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:50.462
Not in the royal household.
They're very happy.

00:08:50.462 --> 00:08:52.564
-Before you go,
I have one question for you.

00:08:52.564 --> 00:08:54.833
I want you to answer it
honestly, okay?

00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:57.169
-Okay.
-If I was in England,

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:59.939
am I famous enough
to be on your show?

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:02.909
-Yes, Seth.
-Are you sure?

00:09:02.909 --> 00:09:04.576
It's a British --
It's an English show.

00:09:04.576 --> 00:09:06.545
I know we don't air over there.

00:09:06.545 --> 00:09:08.413
-There's a global pandemic.
We'll have you.

00:09:08.413 --> 00:09:11.016
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:11.016 --> 00:09:14.085
I'm gonna bring my cameraman
Buck to be in the audience.

00:09:14.085 --> 00:09:16.822
-I'm on your show,
so come on.

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:18.958
All bets are off.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.958 --> 00:09:20.159
-Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:22.128
It's always a pleasure
to see you, Graham.

00:09:22.128 --> 00:09:23.863
-All right, take care.
Thank you so much, Seth.

